From a85b3bbab4c5b7b74fc163a28173596a26838d04 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 22:35:25 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] VideoPlayer: pass audio through for live streams, by a
 setting

Since 2015 (126149c45d8, "realtime streams do require special
treatment, audio must be capable of accepting speed adjustments") Kodi
has decoded the audio of a realtime stream (live TV, IPTV) to PCM: for
such a stream VideoPlayer slows the clock by 5% while the audio queue is
below 5% of its time, until it is above 10%, and decoded audio is
resampled to follow. A receiver then got multichannel PCM, and E-AC-3
lost its Atmos.

Kodi itself no longer adjusts the clock for passthrough audio (pannal,
7705225f10, in Piers): the gate is there, but the audio player never let
passthrough reach it. A new setting, "Allow passthrough on live TV and
streams" (audiooutput.hadeslivepassthrough; System > Audio, with the
passthrough settings, shown at the Advanced level while passthrough is
on, default on), lets it: passthrough audio of a live stream is then
kept in sync as that of any other stream (SYNC_DISCON: the clock follows
the audio, and a picture is dropped or repeated), decoded audio of a
live stream is resampled as before, and "sync playback to display"
still rules passthrough out. Nor is the codec made again when the
stream turns out to be realtime: it opens before VideoPlayer marks the
stream realtime, so every live stream opened a passthrough codec only to
replace it at its first frame, and a timeshift that ends or starts
switched between the two.

The cost, from the code (not measured), which the help of the setting
says plainly: there is no 5% slow-down, so a source whose clock runs
slower than the output's empties the buffer at the difference of the
two (20 to 80 ppm is 72 to 288 ms an hour) and ends in a rebuffer of
about a second (the queues filling to 10%) instead of slowed,
lower-pitched sound; the margin for network jitter is the preroll of
Kodi (the engine's cache plus 400 ms), without the slow-downs that can
grow it to 10% of the queue. Off, every rule is Kodi's as it was: no
passthrough for a realtime stream or while the audio is resampled, and
the codec swap.

The rules are LIVEAUDIO (LivePassthrough.h), and CVideoPlayerAudio reads
the setting once when a stream opens (it goes with the codec to the
audio thread) and logs what it does. The strings are core strings in
English (39890, 39891), translated in Estuary (31960, 31961: es_es,
es_ar, es_mx) and shown through CGUIUtils::GetHadesString().

A slow-down that was set while the audio was decoded is undone when the
audio of a live stream switches to passthrough, which a display reset
can now do: passthrough cannot follow it, and the clock would drift
50 ms a second from the audio until a correction of 100 ms reset it.
With the setting off a live stream cannot switch to passthrough, so
nothing there changes.

Host test (live_passthrough_test.cpp): every combination of the setting,
sync to display, a realtime stream, a codec that passes through and
audio that is resampled, against the rules as VideoPlayerAudio.cpp had
them (off) and against what the setting is for (on), and the life of a
live stream opened before it is marked realtime: off, a passthrough
codec and then a decoding one; on, one passthrough codec. Ten mutations
of the rules make it fail.

Idea from the YBLOD fork, 4fab322c23; rewritten.
